# Minimal Suricata signatures for the lab. The action keyword decides the mode:
#   - IDS (af-packet `-i eth0`): drop/reject are DOWNGRADED to alert (Suricata is
#     off-path and cannot act), so these still just log to eve.json.
#   - IPS (NFQUEUE `-q 0`, on-path via the nfq-init sidecar): the actions BITE —
#     `reject` forges a TCP RST (→ userspace-TCP rst_rate), `drop` silently blocks
#     (→ connect_timeout_rate / retransmit_rate). This is what makes the flow-level
#     RL state axis (rst_rate/connect_timeout_rate/…) go NON-ZERO on a custom_tcp
#     corpus — an inline IPS reaction the HTTP-status defense signals cannot see.
# The action mix is deliberate: reject (RST) and drop (timeout) exercise different
# flow counters so the corpus spans both connection-layer reaction shapes.

# High-rate HTTP from a single source — the load itself, seen as a flood. RST it
# (reject) once over threshold → rst_rate signal.
reject http any any -> any any ( \
    msg:"SAMURAI-LAB HTTP request flood (per-source rate)"; \
    flow:to_server; \
    threshold:type both, track by_src, count 500, seconds 5; \
    classtype:attempted-dos; sid:9000001; rev:1;)

# Scanner user-agents (mirrors the WAF scanner rule at the network layer). RST it.
reject http any any -> any any ( \
    msg:"SAMURAI-LAB scanner user-agent"; \
    flow:to_server; http.user_agent; \
    pcre:"/(?:sqlmap|nikto|nmap|masscan|dirbuster|nuclei|acunetix)/i"; \
    classtype:web-application-attack; sid:9000002; rev:1;)

# Burst of new connections — connection-flood shape (SYN pressure at L7 ingress).
# Silently drop the excess → connect_timeout_rate / retransmit_rate signal.
drop tcp any any -> any 8080 ( \
    msg:"SAMURAI-LAB connection flood"; \
    flags:S; \
    threshold:type both, track by_src, count 300, seconds 5; \
    classtype:attempted-dos; sid:9000003; rev:1;)
